<p>I discovered another person to follow this week, and as I reached out via email, I realized this had become somewhat of a habit for me: writing emails to other bloggers. I&rsquo;m not sure when I started doing this more, but I&rsquo;ve been doing it more in the last year or so.</p>
<p>Other folks who blog, especially those who open up a bit more and talk about their personal lives, is something I appreciate. I know it&rsquo;s not an easy thing to do, especially if there are some contradictions between personal and professional life. For a while, that was my situation as well. Not to say I&rsquo;m completely open about my personal life on my blog, but in general I feel I can be more authentic there, and I&rsquo;d like to think readers can sense that as well. <a href="https://taonaw.com/2024/06/23/blogging-about-blogging.html">I said before that blogging about blogging is writing about yourself</a>; well, this applies here too.</p>
<p>Reaching out by email is something more intimate and personal than a comment. That&rsquo;s because it&rsquo;s a 1:1 conversation and tends to be a longer, more thoughtful exchange. I think every email I sent received a response. That in itself is a reason to write. People open up, tell me more, and exchange information. I enjoy getting ot know them, as a loose level of friendship online.</p>
<p>It feels like a privilege, though it shouldn&rsquo;t. I think everyone should be able to write and express themselves online, but most folks don&rsquo;t know how or don&rsquo;t have the means. <a href="https://indieweb.org/">Owning a blog, a website that you have control over</a>, is hard. It shouldn&rsquo;t be.</p>
